# Private car ownership over different years. The numbers other than years are
# percentages, and should be integers (i.e., no decimal point).
#
# This is indexed by passenger class (as of July 2017). 0 is the lowest and 4 the highest.
# From 2002 the five classes are fed from the five household income quintiles of the
# National Travel Survey (class 0 = lowest quintile, class 4 = highest). The measure is the
# proportion of households in the quintile with access to one or more cars or vans, i.e.
# 100 minus the published "no car or van" percentage.
#
# The classes are era-relative social ranks, not strict income quintiles: class 4 is narrower
# than the highest quintile, so its rate is an approximation.
#
# Household access is used rather than personal access. NTS0704 "Personal car access by
# household income quintile" gives higher figures (2025: 63, 78, 87, 87, 87) because more than
# one adult can use one household car. The household measure counts one car per household and
# is the basis this file has used since 2002; changing to NTS0704 would raise every class.
#
# Sources
#   2002-2025: National Travel Survey table NTS0703, "Household car availability by household
#     income quintile: England, 2002 onwards", Department for Transport, release of
#     10 September 2026 (NTS 2025).
#     https://www.gov.uk/government/statistical-data-sets/nts07-car-ownership-and-access
#   1951-2001: the general vehicle-availability series for Great Britain in DfT Transport
#     Statistics Great Britain 2008, section 9 (vehicles), is the anchor. No measured quintile
#     series for this period was found; the class rows are extrapolations.
#     http://webarchive.nationalarchives.gov.uk/20091009084502/http://www.dft.gov.uk/pgr/statistics/datatablespublications/tsgb/2008edition/sectionninevehicles.pdf
#   Cross-check on the low-income end: RAC Foundation, "Low income motoring" (Bayliss, 2009):
#     http://www.racfoundation.org/assets/rac_foundation/content/downloadables/low_income_motoring-bayliss-280909.pdf
#   Before 1951: educated guesses.
#
# The National Travel Survey covers England; the older TSGB series covered Great Britain.
# Other countries might vary. NTS values are revised with each release: the 2009, 2013 and
# 2022 entries here follow the 2026 release, not the release copied into this file in 2024, and
# the years missing from that copy (2008, 2009, 2016 and 2017) are now filled in. The
# 2020-2022 figures were affected by the COVID-19 change in survey method and by small
# samples (3,050-4,796 households); the 2020 values are a published result, not a typing
# error.
#
# 2030 and 2050 continue the measured 2002-2025 trajectory with a logarithmically decaying
# rate: the annual change starts at the measured 2002-2025 average, (2025 value - 2002 value)
# / 23, and halves over the 25 years to 2050 (rate proportional to 1 / (1 + t / 25)). That
# puts 19.8% of the measured change in the first five years and 75.3% in the 25.
#
# A class is not allowed to pass a higher class. Where a projected value would exceed the
# class above, it stops at equality with that class instead. Classes 2, 3 and 4 are all at
# 83% in 2025, so their shared trajectory stops there and all three hold at 83 in 2030 and
# 2050. Classes 0 and 1 are unaffected and move to 57 and 72 in 2030 and 60 and 76 in 2050.
# Both projected points are estimates, not measurements.
#
# GAMEPLAY ASSUMPTION, 2026-11-18: classes 0 and 1 carry 1% before 1945 so that the pre-war
# motorcycle-and-sidecar outfits can spawn at all. This is NOT a historical ownership rate. The
# research of 2026-11-18 established licensed vehicle counts (including 132,309 sidecar outfits in
# 1935) but no ownership rate by class for cars, motorcycles or outfits before 1945; the earlier
# zeros were themselves assumptions. The direction is supported (outfits cost GBP 77-88 new in 1925
# against GBP 141 for an Austin Seven, and working-class motoring existed by the late 1930s), the
# magnitude is not. Class 1 runs at 1% from 1925 and 2% by 1945; class 0 at 1% from 1940. See
# New/ai/objects/citycar-wealth-classes.md.
#
# Author: James E. Petts (jamespetts)

car_ownership[0]=1750,0,1940,1,1945,1,1955,2,1960,5,1965,7,1973,10,1980,18,1983,20,1992,30,1995,34,2002,51,2003,53,2004,56,2005,56,2006,57,2007,54,2008,51,2009,48,2010,52,2011,52,2012,54,2013,52,2014,53,2015,52,2016,56,2017,56,2018,54,2019,55,2020,65,2021,62,2022,60,2023,56,2024,60,2025,56,2030,57,2050,60
